1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,761
Shannon capacity is the speed limit — the one number no radio, no protocol, and no

2
00:00:07,761 --> 00:00:09,938
clever engineer will ever beat.

3
00:00:09,938 --> 00:00:15,488
Before 1948, engineers assumed noise simply meant errors: push data faster, get

4
00:00:15,488 --> 00:00:17,034
more of them, forever.

5
00:00:17,034 --> 00:00:20,757
Then Claude Shannon proved something nobody expected.

6
00:00:20,757 --> 00:00:26,588
Every noisy channel has an exact capacity — a rate below which communication can be

7
00:00:26,588 --> 00:00:30,663
made as error-free as you like, and above which it cannot.

8
00:00:30,663 --> 00:00:32,700
Not difficult above the line.

9
00:00:32,700 --> 00:00:33,473
Impossible.

10
00:00:33,923 --> 00:00:36,776
The whole result comes from two resources.

11
00:00:36,776 --> 00:00:42,211
First, bandwidth — how wide the channel is, in hertz — sets how many independent

12
00:00:42,211 --> 00:00:44,384
symbols per second it can carry.

13
00:00:44,384 --> 00:00:49,819
Second, signal-to-noise ratio — how far the signal stands above the noise — sets

14
00:00:49,819 --> 00:00:55,253
how many distinguishable levels each symbol can reliably hold, and therefore how

15
00:00:55,253 --> 00:00:57,087
many bits each one encodes.

16
00:00:57,087 --> 00:00:59,057
Width, times bits per symbol.

17
00:00:59,057 --> 00:01:01,843
That is everything a channel has to give.

18
00:01:02,293 --> 00:01:07,664
Written down, the ceiling is one line: capacity C equals bandwidth B, times the log

19
00:01:07,664 --> 00:01:12,453
base two of one plus the signal-to-noise ratio — bits per second, from two

20
00:01:12,453 --> 00:01:13,682
measurable numbers.

21
00:01:13,682 --> 00:01:18,795
Divide through by bandwidth and you get spectral efficiency: the ceiling on how

22
00:01:18,795 --> 00:01:22,030
many bits each hertz of spectrum can ever deliver.

23
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:26,189
The shape of that curve explains half of radio engineering.

24
00:01:26,189 --> 00:01:31,407
At high signal-to-noise, capacity grows only logarithmically: each extra three dB —

25
00:01:31,407 --> 00:01:36,185
a doubling of signal power — buys roughly one more bit per second per hertz.

26
00:01:36,185 --> 00:01:39,454
Brute-force power hits brutally diminishing returns.

27
00:01:39,454 --> 00:01:44,672
At low signal-to-noise, the curve is nearly linear — so you can trade bandwidth for

28
00:01:44,672 --> 00:01:49,953
power, spreading a weak signal across more hertz and still getting the bits through.

29
00:01:49,953 --> 00:01:54,479
That trade is the principle behind spread-spectrum and deep-space links.

30
00:01:54,479 --> 00:01:58,817
And there is an absolute floor: below about minus one point six dB of

31
00:01:58,817 --> 00:02:03,909
energy-per-bit against noise density, reliable communication is impossible at any

32
00:02:03,909 --> 00:02:04,538
bandwidth.

33
00:02:04,988 --> 00:02:07,542
Here's the strangest part of the story.

34
00:02:07,542 --> 00:02:12,977
Shannon proved capacity is achievable — codes exist that approach it with vanishing

35
00:02:12,977 --> 00:02:18,413
error — but his proof was non-constructive: it offered no recipe for building them.

36
00:02:18,413 --> 00:02:23,128
The gap between the proven limit and working hardware is what decades of

37
00:02:23,128 --> 00:02:28,498
error-correction research have been closing, and today's best codes — turbo codes,

38
00:02:28,498 --> 00:02:33,672
LDPC — operate within a fraction of a decibel of the line Shannon drew in 1948.

39
00:02:34,122 --> 00:02:39,192
Why this matters to a listener: every modulation-and-coding scheme in every modern

40
00:02:39,192 --> 00:02:44,078
standard is, in effect, a chosen point on Shannon's curve — a trade of spectral

41
00:02:44,078 --> 00:02:45,933
efficiency against robustness.

42
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:47,850
And the theorem cuts both ways.

43
00:02:47,850 --> 00:02:52,797
If the signal-to-noise at your antenna sits below what a waveform's chosen point

44
00:02:52,797 --> 00:02:57,744
requires, no receiver cleverness will recover the data — the transmitter already

45
00:02:57,744 --> 00:03:01,639
sent at a rate your channel cannot sustain at your noise level.

46
00:03:01,639 --> 00:03:06,648
The only honest lever is the ratio itself: a better antenna, a quieter front end,

47
00:03:06,648 --> 00:03:07,328
less noise.

48
00:03:07,778 --> 00:03:13,124
GopherTrunk never computes capacity, but its entire decode chain lives under this

49
00:03:13,124 --> 00:03:13,651
theorem.

50
00:03:13,651 --> 00:03:18,931
When the SNR readout sits above what a waveform's coding and modulation require,

51
00:03:18,931 --> 00:03:21,372
frames decode and the counters climb.

52
00:03:21,372 --> 00:03:26,784
When it sits below, they don't — and no setting changes that, because the boundary

53
00:03:26,784 --> 00:03:29,687
is exactly the line Shannon's formula draws.

54
00:03:29,687 --> 00:03:34,702
Watch the SNR number next to a live decode, and you are watching the theorem

55
00:03:34,702 --> 00:03:35,692
enforce itself.

56
00:03:36,142 --> 00:03:41,297
So this is where every radio conversation ends: bandwidth and signal-to-noise,

57
00:03:41,297 --> 00:03:45,658
multiplied into a hard ceiling that no code or modulation exceeds.

58
00:03:45,658 --> 00:03:51,077
Capacity equals bandwidth times log two of one plus SNR; at high SNR each three dB

59
00:03:51,077 --> 00:03:56,495
buys about one more bit per hertz; and below the SNR a signal requires, no decoder

60
00:03:56,495 --> 00:03:57,288
can save it.

61
00:03:57,288 --> 00:04:02,508
That ceiling is the wall every protocol in this course is climbing toward — and

62
00:04:02,508 --> 00:04:06,606
everything a radio does well is a way of getting closer to it.

63
00:04:06,606 --> 00:04:08,390
Full write-up linked below.

